<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Intentionally empty. Do not add entitlements here without checking the app actually needs
         them under a Developer ID signature.

         This file used to declare:
             keychain-access-groups = [ $(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.shogun.spike ]
         which did nothing and broke the shipped app. Two reasons:

         1. `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` is an Xcode build variable. `codesign` does not expand build
            variables, so the signed binary carried the literal string. `keychain-access-groups` is
            a restricted entitlement, so AMFI refused to start the hardened-runtime app: the
            process took SIGKILL at exec and Finder reported only "ShogunAI can't be opened".
         2. Access groups apply to the data-protection keychain. keychain_store.rs stores
            kSecClassGenericPassword items in the default file keychain, where access is decided by
            ACLs and the caller's code signature — the group was never consulted.

         Everything this app asks of the OS is TCC-gated, not entitlement-gated: Accessibility,
         Screen Recording and the microphone are granted by the user against the bundle identifier,
         with the purpose strings in Info.plist. Non-sandboxed Developer ID apps need no entitlement
         for network access or for their own keychain items. -->
</dict>
</plist>
